  "chapter": "Chapter 3. Insurance and Indemnification",
  "section": "3.5. Commercial General Liability (CGL)",
  "subsection": "E. Commercial General Liability (CGL) Insurance",
  "text": "(4) [3:316] Criminal acts: “Personal injury” claims “arising out of a criminal act committed by or at the direction of any insured” are specifically excluded. [CG 00 01 12 07, Sec. I, Coverage B, ¶ 2.d.] (5) [3:317] False publication: A similar exclusion applies to “personal injury” claims arising out of publication of material “if done by or at the direction of the insured with knowledge of its falsity.” [CG 00 01 12 07, Sec. I, Coverage B, ¶ 2.b.] (6) [3:318] Compare—Employment-Related Practices Exclusion endorsement: Although the standard CGL form does not expressly exclude damages arising from “employment-related practices,” such an exclusion is available through an Employment-Related Practices Exclusion endorsement (ISO form CG 21 47 07 98). This endorsement excludes claims for “bodily injury” or “personal and advertising injury” arising out of “any refusal to employ …; termination of employment; or 3 employment-related practices, policies, acts or omissions, such as coercion, demotion, evaluation, reassignment, discipline, defamation, harassment, humiliation or discrimination …” [See Golden Eagle Ins. Corp. v.",
